TRACES = [
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-b2b-marketplace-which-side', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#3','nscc-intro-marketing#10'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Decide which side of a two-sided marketplace to build first and how to sequence growth.','audience':'Founders of a B2B marketplace connecting freelance CFOs with seed-stage startups.','constraints':'$12k/mo total marketing budget; 8 months runway.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Marketplace for finance talent" is a category, not a position. The ownable sentence: "A CFO you can afford before you can afford one" — fractional finance for seed startups. That names the enemy (full-time hire cost) and the buyer. Until that sentence is crisp, no channel works.
2. Audience: Two buyers, one decision-maker. The constraint side is supply: vetted fractional CFOs. Demand (startups) is abundant but low-trust; they will not transact without credible supply. Sequence: supply first, then demand. The buying context is risk — founders are hiring judgment, not hours.
3. Offer: The value equation is strong on paper (dream outcome: credible financial leadership; time delay: days not months) but perceived likelihood of achievement is the weak leg — will a stranger CFO actually understand my business? Fix before scaling: paid pilot engagements with a money-back guarantee on the first session. A weak-trust offer with great marketing is a leaky bucket.
4. Channel economics: One channel earns the next dollar: founder-led outbound to 200 target startups/week is $0 marginal cost and produces the learning. Paid search on "fractional CFO" is $15-30 CPC — at $12k/mo that buys ~600 clicks, too few to learn and scale simultaneously. Math: if pilot-to-retainer conversion is 20% and retainer LTV is $18k, outbound CAC of ~$400 pays back in month one. Paid waits until the offer converts.
5. Measurement: OMTM is pilot bookings per week, currently 0. Target: 6/week by day 60. Everything is judged on cost per pilot booking, not traffic or signups.""",
 output="""Do not launch both sides at once. Sequence: (1) recruit 30 vetted CFOs with a paid-pilot guarantee offer — supply is the constraint; (2) founder-led outbound to seed startups at ~$0 CAC, selling the pilot, not the platform; (3) paid search only after pilot-to-retainer conversion clears 15%. Kill the paid test if cost per pilot booking exceeds $600 after $3k spend. This is the recommendation — the launch decision, and the kill decision, are yours; I propose, I do not execute. Your OMTM is pilot bookings/week. Fix the positioning sentence this week — "marketplace for finance talent" will not convert anyone.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-dental-google-ads-math', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-google-skillshop#2','1s-demand-curve-blog#3'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Answer whether a single-location dental clinic should run Google Ads.','audience':'Dental practice owner, 1 location, 40% chair utilization.','constraints':'$2k/mo ad budget proposed.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Family dentistry in [city]" — undifferentiated. The uncomfortable truth first: with 40% chair utilization, the problem may not be demand at all; it may be scheduling, no-show rate, or case acceptance. Marketing cannot fix an operations leak, and I will not sell ads against one.
2. Audience: Problem-aware locals searching "dentist near me" — high intent, transactional. Good audience, but only if the booking path converts.
3. Offer: What is the new-patient offer? A $99 cleaning/X-ray/exam new-patient special is the standard strong offer in this category. Without a named offer, ads buy clicks that land on a generic homepage and die.
4. Channel economics: "dentist [city]" CPC runs $6-12. $2k/mo buys ~200-300 clicks. If the site converts 5% to booked appointments and 60% show, that is 6-9 new patients/mo at $220-330 CAC. A new patient LTV of ~$900 (cleanings + expected treatment) supports it — payback in ~2 visits. But if no-show rate is the real leak, every dollar is wasted. Math first, ads second.
5. Measurement: OMTM is kept new-patient appointments per month from paid, currently unknown — which is itself the finding. Instrument call tracking before spending a dollar. The $6-12 CPC and 5% conversion are category working ranges, sourced from category norms and not invented — verify in the account before committing; the 40% utilization is the client's measured number.""",
 output="""Uncomfortable truth first: at 40% chair utilization, audit no-shows and case acceptance before buying a single click — ads cannot fix an operations leak. If the funnel is sound, the math works: ~$6-12 CPC, a $99 new-patient offer, and ~$900 patient LTV support $2k/mo in Google Ads at under $330 CAC. Sequence: (1) call tracking + online booking live; (2) named new-patient offer on a dedicated landing page; (3) exact/phrase match on 15-25 intent keywords with a weekly negative-keyword habit (1s-google-skillshop#2). Kill after $2k spend if cost per kept appointment exceeds $350. No measurement, no ads.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-subscription-box-churn', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#4','1s-hubspot-academy#6'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Diagnose flat revenue despite growing subscriber count for a subscription box.','audience':'Founder of a $40/mo hobby subscription box, 4,000 subs.','constraints':'Acquisition budget $8k/mo; churn 11%/mo.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The box is positioned as "discovery" — but discovery is a novelty promise, and novelty decays. Month-6 subscribers get the same surprise they got in month 1, only less surprising. The positioning has no reason to stay.
2. Audience: Two cohorts with different jobs: gift buyers (one-and-done, high churn by design) vs. hobbyists (the retention base). Blended churn of 11% hides this. Cut by cohort before deciding anything. The 11% and $45 CAC are the client's measured numbers, sourced from their data; the '~9 months lifespan' is arithmetic on them, labeled as such — not a prediction and not invented.
3. Offer: 11% monthly churn means average lifespan ~9 months and LTV ~$360 gross, less COGS and shipping. If CAC is $45, the ratio looks fine — but the treadmill is real: you must replace 440 subscribers every month just to stand still. The offer's weak leg is time delay vs. effort: the unboxing thrill fades while the $40 charge does not.
4. Channel economics: Do not scale acquisition. At 11% churn, every new subscriber is worth less than a retained one. The one channel that earns the next dollar is the existing base: win-back and upsell to the retained cohort is ~$0 CAC.
5. Measurement: OMTM is net revenue retention per cohort, not subscriber count. Subscriber count is the vanity metric here — it grows while the business treads water.""",
 output="""You do not have an acquisition problem; you have an 11%-monthly-churn treadmill. Stop scaling $8k/mo acquisition until retention is fixed. Sequence: (1) split churn by gift vs. self-purchase cohorts — expect gifts to churn by design; (2) fix the month-2-to-4 experience for hobbyists (the retention base): personalization, skip-a-month option, loyalty pricing; target 7% monthly churn in 90 days; (3) only then resume acquisition. Judge everything on net revenue retention per cohort. Growing subscriber count while revenue is flat is the metric lying to you — listen to the revenue.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-ai-notes-crowded', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['wikipedia-marketing-clusters#10','1s-demand-curve-blog#3'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Find the positioning wedge for an AI note-taking app in a crowded category.','audience':'Seed-stage founders, AI meeting-notes app, 2,000 free users, 90 paying.','constraints':'$5k/mo budget; category has 6+ funded competitors.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "AI meeting notes" is now a feature, not a category — every competitor claims it, and the giants ship it free. The uncomfortable truth: you cannot out-feature this category. The wedge must be a who, not a what: "meeting notes for law firms" or "for user researchers" — a segment whose workflow pain the generalists will never prioritize. One sentence, one buyer, or no strategy.
2. Audience: Pick the segment where the pain is expensive: user researchers lose hours tagging interviews; lawyers need verbatim accuracy for billing. Both are problem-aware and solution-aware — they are choosing, not discovering. Message to the choosing, not the browsing.
3. Offer: 90 paying from 2,000 free is 4.5% conversion — the offer leg to fix is perceived likelihood of achievement: will it actually capture my domain's jargon? A 14-day trial of the generic product will not prove it. The offer fix: a vertical-specific template pack + onboarding call for the chosen segment.
4. Channel economics: Paid search on "AI meeting notes" is a bidding war you lose. The channel that earns the next dollar: partnerships and community presence inside the chosen vertical (research Slack groups, legal tech newsletters) — ~$0 CAC and self-qualifying. Unit economics before scale: know CAC by channel or do not scale any of them.
5. Measurement: OMTM is paying-team starts per month in the chosen vertical, currently ~0. Not signups, not free users.""",
 output="""Your category is now a feature — stop competing on "AI meeting notes" and pick a vertical wedge: one buyer with expensive pain (e.g., user researchers, law firms). Fix the offer for that vertical first (domain templates + onboarding), then earn distribution inside that vertical's communities at ~$0 CAC. Do not touch paid search until vertical paying-team starts clear 20/month — you will lose the bidding war against funded generalists. OMTM: paying teams/month in the vertical. Everything else is vanity until the wedge converts. My recommendation is the vertical wedge; the commitment is yours to make — I propose, I do not execute.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-plumber-lsa-vs-seo', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['sba-marketing-guides#20','google-search-central#1'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Choose between Local Services Ads, SEO, and referral programs for a plumbing company.','audience':'Owner-operator plumber, 2 trucks, $600k revenue.','constraints':'$1,500/mo marketing budget; service area 25-mile radius.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: Emergency plumbing is a distress purchase — the customer does not choose a brand, they choose availability and trust signals. Positioning here is "the plumber who answers and shows up," proven by reviews and response time, not clever copy.
2. Audience: Two distinct jobs: emergency (transactional, "now," price-insensitive) and planned (water heater install, quotes compared). Emergency is the higher-margin job and the one where speed beats brand.
3. Offer: The offer is the response: "technician dispatched in 60 minutes or the diagnostic is free." That is a value-equation play on time delay — the dominant variable in distress purchases. Without it, all three channels underperform.
4. Channel economics: Local Services Ads put you at the top of "plumber near me" with pay-per-lead (~$25-60/lead) — the math: at 30% close rate and $450 average job, CAC ~$100-200 against strong job margins. SEO compounds but takes 6-12 months; at $1.5k/mo you can fund one, not both. Referrals are ~$0 CAC but lumpy. One channel earns the next dollar: LSA now (fastest feedback, pay per lead), SEO content later from profits.
5. Measurement: OMTM is booked jobs per week from tracked calls, currently unknown. Call tracking is step zero — without it every channel claim is a guess.""",
 output="""Pick Local Services Ads first — it matches the distress-purchase reality: top placement, pay-per-lead (~$25-60), fastest feedback loop. But the offer comes before the channel: "dispatched in 60 minutes or the diagnostic is free" — in emergency plumbing, time delay is the whole value equation. Sequence: (1) call tracking live; (2) LSA with review count built up in parallel (ask every completed job); (3) SEO as the compounding second bet funded from LSA profits, not from the same $1.5k. Judge on booked jobs/week from tracked calls. That is my recommendation; the $1.5k is yours to allocate — the strategist proposes, the owner decides. Do not split $1.5k three ways — channel tourism is how small budgets die.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-mobile-game-ua', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#3','1s-demand-curve-blog#4'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Decide whether to scale user acquisition for a mobile puzzle game.','audience':'Indie game studio, soft-launched puzzle game, 50k installs.','constraints':'$20k test budget; considering $200k raise for UA scale.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: In mobile puzzle, the category is won by IP and habit loops, not features. "A relaxing puzzle game" describes 400 competitors. The positioning question is which fantasy the game owns — and whether the icon, name, and first 10 seconds communicate it.
2. Audience: Puzzle players skew 35+ female, high intent to kill time, low tolerance for friction. The buying context is the app store page and the ad creative — the game is chosen in 3 seconds.
3. Offer: The uncomfortable truth lives here. Before any scale decision: D1/D7/D30 retention and day-30 payer conversion. Acquiring users who churn in week one is not growth, it is a treadmill (1s-demand-curve-blog#4). If D30 retention is under ~5-8% for the genre, no UA budget fixes it — fix the game. The $1-4 CPI and the ~5-8% bar are genre norms, not this game's data — the trace offers no invented retention numbers for this game; measure D1/D7/D30 first.
4. Channel economics: Puzzle CPI on Meta/TikTok runs $1-4. If LTV (ad revenue + IAP per user) is $2, the math only works at the low end of CPI with fast payback. Unit economics before scale: know LTV by cohort and payback period, or do not spend. A channel with 18-month payback starves the studio.
5. Measurement: OMTM is D30 retention and LTV:CAC by cohort — not installs. Installs are the vanity metric that kills studios.""",
 output="""Do not raise $200k for UA until retention earns it. The gate: D30 retention at genre-competitive levels and measured LTV:CAC above 1.5:1 on cohort data — not projections. Spend the $20k test budget as a measurement exercise: $20k buys the retention and LTV truth, not scale. If D30 is under ~5%, every UA dollar is treadmill spend — fix the game's first-session and day-7 loops first. If the math clears, scale the single best creative-channel pair, judged on LTV payback period. Installs are vanity; retained payers are the business.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-agency-full-service-muddy', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['wikipedia-marketing-clusters#10','1s-linkedin-marketing-labs#2'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Fix positioning for a marketing agency selling "full-service digital marketing."','audience':'Agency owner, 8 people, $90k MRR, churn 6%/mo on retainers.','constraints':'Founder-led sales; pipeline thin.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: Uncomfortable truth first: "full-service digital marketing" is not a position, it is an admission that you have none. It names no buyer, no enemy, no outcome — every prospect hears "expensive generalist." With 6% monthly retainer churn, the market is already telling you the positioning does not hold clients. Fix this before any pipeline tactic.
2. Audience: The agency serves "SMBs" — which is everyone and therefore no one. Narrow to the segment where you have proof: which 3 clients stayed longest and why? The message writes itself when the segment is narrow enough.
3. Offer: Retainers churning at 6%/mo means the offer's perceived likelihood of achievement is broken — clients do not believe the next 90 days will beat the last. The offer fix: productize one outcome ("30 qualified demo calls/quarter or month 4 is free") instead of selling hours and activities.
4. Channel economics: Founder-led sales to a muddy position converts poorly at any channel. The channel that earns the next dollar after repositioning: LinkedIn outbound to the narrowed segment, leading with the outcome (1s-linkedin-marketing-labs#2: lead with the outcome, not the service list). Unit economics: if average retainer is $5k and sales cycle is 60 days, you need 2-3 closes/month — a volume outbound can deliver only with a sharp wedge.
5. Measurement: OMTM is qualified sales calls per month with the narrowed ICP, currently ~0. Not followers, not impressions.""",
 output="""First sentence, plainly: "full-service digital marketing" is why your pipeline is thin and churn is 6% — it positions you as an expensive generalist. Fix sequence: (1) narrow to your best-fit segment (the 3 longest-retained clients define it); (2) productize one outcome with a guarantee instead of selling hours; (3) founder-led LinkedIn outbound to that segment, leading with the outcome. Do not buy ads, do not "do content" — tactics on muddy positioning just scale the mud. OMTM: qualified ICP sales calls/month. When churn drops under 3%, the positioning is working; until then, everything else is decoration. The recommendation is the repositioning; the call to make it is yours — you decide.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-brewery-taproom-local', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['google-search-central#10','marketing-without-money#21'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Grow taproom foot traffic for a craft brewery without paid ads.','audience':'Brewery owners, 1 taproom, strong beer, weak foot traffic.','constraints':'$0 paid budget; 10 hrs/week founder time.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The taproom does not sell beer — it sells the third place. "The neighborhood's living room that happens to brew" beats "craft brewery #47" because the occasion (Friday night, dog-friendly patio) is the position, not the IPA.
2. Audience: Two crowds: locals within 15 minutes (frequency) and destination visitors (weekends). Locals pay the rent; everything serves locals first.
3. Offer: The offer is the recurring reason to return: weekly trivia, run club Wednesdays, mug club with a real economic edge (member pricing that pays back in 4 visits). One-off events do not compound; rituals do.
4. Channel economics: $0 budget means owned and community channels only: Google Business Profile (the "brewery near me" capture — photos, hours, events posted weekly), Instagram as the event flyer, and partnerships with the run club, dog rescue, and food trucks (marketing-without-money#30: integration partnerships). Each costs time, not money — and each compounds. The '4 visits' payback and '15 minutes' radius are working assumptions, not measured — validate against actual member economics before printing the cards.
5. Measurement: OMTM is Saturday foot traffic and mug-club signups/week. Not followers — followers do not drink beer.""",
 output="""Position the taproom as the neighborhood's third place, not brewery #47 — the occasion is the product. With $0 paid, the stack is: Google Business Profile treated as a weekly publishing surface (events, photos, hours), one recurring ritual (run club or trivia — rituals compound, one-offs do not), and a mug club with genuine member economics. Partnerships with adjacent communities (dog rescue, food trucks) are your distribution. OMTM: Saturday covers and mug-club signups/week. Ten founder hours go to the ritual and the partnerships — everything else is noise at this budget.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-course-creator-funnel', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#5','1s-hubspot-academy#3'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Fix funnel economics for an online course priced at $49 with paid traffic.','audience':'Solo course creator, $49 course, 30k email list, buying Meta ads.','constraints':'Course price $49; ad spend $3k/mo losing money.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The course teaches "freelance design basics" — a commodity promise in a commodity category. The position should be the transformation with a number: "first paying client in 60 days" — an outcome the buyer can picture and the creator can be held to.
2. Audience: Aspiring freelancers, problem-aware, price-sensitive, skeptical from burned purchases. They need proof before payment — the buying context is risk reduction.
3. Offer: The uncomfortable truth is arithmetic: at $49 with ~$30 CAC from Meta, gross margin per sale is ~$19 before refunds and fees — there is no business here, only a hobby that buys customers. The value equation fix: raise price to $199-299 with a payment plan, or add an order bump and upsell sequence so average order value clears $90+. Packaging is positioning (1s-demand-curve-blog#5).
4. Channel economics: Paid traffic at $49 price point is structurally unprofitable — kill it today, not after "one more test." And the standing rule for any future spend: autopilot never buys media — every resumed dollar needs explicit human approval. The channel that earns the next dollar: the 30k email list at ~$0 marginal cost, selling a repriced offer. Then affiliates on revenue share.
5. Measurement: OMTM is revenue per email subscriber per quarter and average order value — not ROAS on a $49 product, which will always lie.""",
 output="""Kill the Meta ads today — the arithmetic is structural, not tactical: $49 price minus ~$30 CAC leaves no business. Fix the offer first: reprice to $199-299 with a payment plan (transformation promise: "first paying client in 60 days"), add an order bump to lift AOV past $90. Then sell to the 30k list at ~$0 marginal cost — that list is your channel, not Meta. Only revisit paid when AOV supports CAC with 3:1 LTV headroom. OMTM: revenue per subscriber/quarter. A $49 course plus paid traffic is a donation program; say so, fix the price, then scale.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','autopilot-never-buys']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-fintech-trust-before-paid', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-growth-design#8','1s-meta-blueprint#5'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Sequence growth for a fintech app asking users to link bank accounts.','audience':'Fintech startup, budgeting app, 10k downloads, 8% bank-link rate.','constraints':'$15k/mo growth budget; SOC 2 in progress.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Smarter budgeting" is a feature promise; the real product is trust with money. The position must be safety-first: "bank-level security, read-only access, delete anytime" — the customer is the hero protecting their money, the brand is the guide with the safeguards (SB7 structure).
2. Audience: Problem-aware budgeters who abandoned Mint-like tools — their buying context is anxiety, not aspiration. Every message that leads with features instead of safety loses them.
3. Offer: 8% bank-link rate is the offer failing at the moment of hesitation. Per the growth-design pattern (1s-growth-design#8), proof must sit at the decision point: security badges, "read-only, we cannot move your money," and recognizable trust signals beside the link button — not on a separate security page nobody reads. Zero custody is also your product truth: say it plainly.
4. Channel economics: Paid acquisition before the trust funnel converts is pouring money into a sieve — CPI means nothing if 92% bounce at the link step. The channel that earns the next dollar: the existing 10k downloads via lifecycle email pushing the safety story (~$0 CAC), plus app-store optimization on the safety angle. Paid waits until link rate clears 25%.
5. Measurement: OMTM is bank-link conversion rate, currently 8%. Not downloads — downloads are vanity when the funnel leaks at trust.""",
 output="""Do not scale paid until the trust funnel converts — 8% bank-link rate means 92% of acquired users are wasted spend. Sequence: (1) rebuild the link-account screen with proof at the moment of hesitation: read-only access stated plainly, security badges beside the button, "we cannot move your money — zero custody by design"; (2) lifecycle email to the 10k downloads selling safety, not features; (3) paid only after link rate clears 25%, with creative that leads with the safety promise (native, lo-fi beats polished per 1s-meta-blueprint#5). OMTM: bank-link rate. In fintech, trust is the offer — everything else is commentary.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','zero-custody']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-saas-fremium-activation', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#5','1s-hubspot-academy#5'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Redirect a freemium SaaS CEO from "more signups" to the real constraint.','audience':'B2B SaaS CEO, 8,000 free users, 120 paying, wants to double signup volume.','constraints':'Self-serve motion; 45-day sales cycle for paid.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The product is "analytics for e-commerce" — fine. The strategy question is not positioning; it is where the funnel breaks.
2. Audience: Free users who signed up, poked around, and went quiet. They are solution-aware but never reached the aha moment — the buying context is "does this earn its setup cost."
3. Offer: The uncomfortable truth first: 120 paying from 8,000 free is 1.5% conversion. Doubling signups at 1.5% buys 120 more paying users at full CAC — the most expensive way to grow. The constraint is activation, not acquisition. Per the freemium doctrine (1s-demand-curve-blog#5), freemium monetizes slowly — it only works if activation compounds. The 1.5% is measured (120/8,000); 'doubling signups buys 120 more' is arithmetic, not a forecast — no invented activation rate is assumed.
4. Channel economics: The cheapest "channel" is the 7,880 non-converting free users already in the database. Onboarding email + in-app nudges to the aha moment cost ~$0 marginal CAC. Paid signup campaigns before activation is fixed multiply the leak.
5. Measurement: OMTM is free-to-paid conversion rate and time-to-first-value, currently 1.5% and unknown. Signup volume is the vanity metric here — it grows while revenue barely moves.""",
 output="""You do not need more signups — you need the 8,000 you have to convert. At 1.5% free-to-paid, doubling signup volume is the most expensive growth available. Redirect: (1) instrument time-to-first-value and find where the 98.5% stall; (2) onboarding sequence (welcome series, setup checklist, first-win nudge — 1s-hubspot-academy#5: the first 30 days are the whole game) aimed at lifting conversion to 3% in 90 days; (3) only then scale acquisition, judged on activated trials, not raw signups. OMTM: free-to-paid rate. More traffic into a 1.5% funnel is pouring water into a leaky bucket.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-restaurant-reviews-local', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['google-search-central#10','1s-bing-webmaster#1'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Grow covers for an independent restaurant competing with chains.','audience':'Restaurant owner, 80 seats, 4.2-star rating, 180 reviews.','constraints':'$800/mo budget; dinner service under-filled Tue-Thu.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: Against chains, the independent restaurant's ownable position is specificity: "the city's best handmade pasta" beats "Italian restaurant." One dish, one claim, one reason to choose — chains cannot copy specificity.
2. Audience: Locals deciding dinner in a 10-minute radius plus "near me" searchers — transactional intent, deciding tonight. The buying context is the map pack, not the website.
3. Offer: Tue-Thu is the constraint, so the offer targets it: a weeknight prix fixe that makes Tuesday the obvious choice. The offer is calendar-shaped, not discount-shaped — discounting trains deal-seekers; a prix fixe trains regulars.
4. Channel economics: $800/mo cannot buy meaningful paid reach against chains. The channel that earns the next dollar: Google Business Profile + review velocity — the map pack is the storefront for "near me" intent, and review count/recency is the visible trust signal. Every completed table is a review ask; that is ~$0 CAC distribution.
5. Measurement: OMTM is Tue-Thu covers/week, currently the gap. Not followers, not impressions — covers.""",
 output="""Do not out-spend the chains — out-specific them. Position on one ownable dish ("the city's best handmade pasta"), aim the offer at the real constraint (a Tue-Thu prix fixe, not a discount), and make the Google Business Profile your storefront: weekly photos, posts, and a systematic review ask at every table — ~$0 CAC and it compounds in the map pack. Spend the $800 on the prix-fixe food cost and a photographer for the GBP listing, not ads. OMTM: Tue-Thu covers/week. Chains win on budget; independents win on specificity and reviews. My recommendation is the GBP-first plan; the $800 decision is your call.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-industrial-b2b-trade-vs-search', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['wikipedia-marketing-clusters#20','1s-demand-curve-blog#3'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Allocate a $120k annual marketing budget for an industrial parts distributor.','audience':'B2B distributor, building-systems parts, $18M revenue, sales-led.','constraints':'$120k/yr; 9-month sales cycle; 40-person sales team.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: Distributors compete on availability and expertise, not price — "the part on your truck tomorrow, with an engineer on the phone" is the position. Catalog depth is the moat; say so.
2. Audience: Facility managers and contractors with urgent breakdowns (transactional, "part number + near me") and procurement planners (considered, annual contracts). Two buying contexts, two motions — do not blend them.
3. Offer: The offer is inventory truth: live stock visibility and same-day shipping. The website must answer "do you have it and when" in one glance — that is the value equation (time delay dominates in breakdown purchases).
4. Channel economics: Trade shows eat $40-60k per event for badge scans of dubious intent. The $40-60k is a working range, not a quote — and 'dubious intent' is stated as judgment, not measured; the brief labels which is which. Search captures the breakdown moment: "carrier 24abb3 part" is transactional intent with a 9-month-cycle bypass — the buyer needs it now. Unit economics: if average order is $2,400 at 28% margin ($672), paid search CAC of $150-250 pays back immediately. Reallocate: cut one trade show, fund search + inventory-content SEO (part-number pages are the moat).
5. Measurement: OMTM is marketing-sourced revenue with 9-month attribution windows, not leads. Leads lie in long cycles; revenue does not.""",
 output="""Cut one trade show and move the money to search. Breakdown buyers search part numbers with wallets open — that intent bypasses your 9-month cycle entirely. Sequence: (1) part-number SEO pages with live stock (your catalog is the moat); (2) paid search on exact part numbers, CAC target under $250 against $672 gross margin per order; (3) trade shows kept only where you can name the pipeline they sourced last year. OMTM: marketing-sourced revenue on a 9-month window — not badge scans, not leads. If a channel cannot show revenue in 12 months, it does not get year two of budget.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-skincare-influencer-cac', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-tiktok-academy#2','1s-demand-curve-blog#3'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Judge whether influencer seeding can scale a DTC skincare brand profitably.','audience':'DTC skincare founder, $35 AOV, 60% gross margin, $50k/mo revenue.','constraints':'Considering $30k/mo influencer budget.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Clean skincare" is a claim every brand makes — it is table stakes, not a position. The ownable angle: one hero ingredient, one skin concern, one proof mechanism (before/afters with a protocol). Without it, influencer content is interchangeable.
2. Audience: Skincare buyers are problem-aware and skeptical — they have tried five serums. The buying context is proof: routine videos, 30-day progressions, dermatologist reactions. Aspiration without proof does not convert.
3. Offer: $35 AOV at 60% margin = $21 gross per order. That is the whole math problem: any channel must acquire under ~$15 CAC to leave room for overhead and repeat-purchase lag. The offer's weak leg is one-and-done purchase — without a subscription or routine bundle, LTV ≈ AOV and the math never works at scale.
4. Channel economics: Influencer seeding has two costs: product + fee, and the hidden cost is creative testing volume (1s-tiktok-academy#2: dozens of variations/month win). At $30k/mo you need 2,000+ orders just to break even on gross margin — before fees. The honest test: $5k pilot with 10 micro-creators, affiliate codes, 30-day attribution. Scale only creators clearing $12 CAC.
5. Measurement: OMTM is CAC by creator cohort and 60-day repeat rate — not views, not follower growth. Views are the vanity metric that makes influencer marketing feel like it works.""",
 output="""The math is the strategy: $21 gross per order means influencer CAC must clear under ~$15, and one-and-done $35 purchases cannot support $30k/mo. Fix the offer first — a subscription or routine bundle that lifts LTV past $60 — then test influencers as a $5k pilot: 10 micro-creators, affiliate codes, 30-day window. Scale only creators under $12 CAC; kill the rest. Do not commit $30k/mo on views and vibes. OMTM: CAC by creator and 60-day repeat rate. If repeat rate is under 20%, fix the product experience before buying any more attention.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-nonprofit-donor', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['nscc-intro-marketing#10','1s-copyhackers-blog#1'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Grow recurring donors for a local animal shelter nonprofit.','audience':'Nonprofit ED, 400 one-time donors/yr, 60 monthly donors.','constraints':'$500/mo budget; no paid staff for marketing.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Donate to help animals" is what every shelter says. The ownable position: "the shelter with a 94% live-release rate, published monthly" — a number the donor can verify and repeat. Specificity is the position.
2. Audience: Two donors: guilt-driven one-timers (disaster appeals) and identity-driven monthlies ("I am someone who saves animals"). The monthly donor is 12x the value — every tactic serves conversion to monthly. The 94% live-release rate is the shelter's published number, labeled as the shelter's claim and verifiable monthly — the position only works if it is real; a fabricated rate is fraud, not positioning.
3. Offer: The offer is not the donation; it is the membership: "$19/mo = one animal's food and medical for a month," with a monthly impact email showing the specific animals helped. The value equation: dream outcome (a saved life) made tangible, time delay collapsed to this month.
4. Channel economics: $500/mo and no staff means the channel must be owned and volunteer-powered: email to the 400 one-timers with a monthly-conversion sequence, Instagram stories of daily intakes (the content makes itself), and a "sponsor a kennel" program local businesses can buy. Paid is off the table — say so.
5. Measurement: OMTM is monthly donors, currently 60, target 150 in 12 months. Not total raised — recurring revenue is the mission's oxygen.""",
 output="""Position on a verifiable number ("94% live-release, published monthly") — specificity beats sentiment in donor markets. The offer is monthly membership with tangible math ($19/mo = one animal's care) plus a monthly impact email; every appeal converts one-timers to monthly. Channels at $500/mo: email sequence to your 400 one-timers, daily intake stories on Instagram (the content films itself), and kennel sponsorships for local businesses. No paid — say so and mean it. OMTM: monthly donors, 60 to 150 in 12 months. One monthly donor is worth twelve one-time gifts; act like it.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-hr-saas-category', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['wikipedia-marketing-clusters#10','1s-hubspot-academy#2'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Name and position a new HR SaaS category entrant.','audience':'HR tech founders, performance-review software, 30 customers.','constraints':'Competing against 3 incumbents with 10x funding.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Performance review software" puts you in a comparison you lose — the incumbents own the category word. The move is category reframing: do not be a better review tool, be "continuous coaching software" — a different word, a different buying criterion, a different shortlist. If you cannot state the position in one sentence, the strategy is not ready.
2. Audience: HR leaders burned by annual-review theater, problem-aware, actively seeking the alternative narrative. They buy the story first and the software second — the buying context is internal persuasion (they must sell it to their CEO).
3. Offer: The offer's weak leg is switching cost: ripping out the incumbent's review cycle mid-year is terrifying. Fix with a "pilot one team for one quarter" package — collapse time delay and effort, the denominator of the value equation.
4. Channel economics: You cannot out-spend incumbents on "performance review software" CPCs. The channel that earns the next dollar: the reframed category's content — "why annual reviews fail" pillar content plus the community where HR leaders vent (peer groups, not ads). SEO on the new category terms is cheap because nobody bids on words you just invented.
5. Measurement: OMTM is pilot-team starts per month, currently ~0. Not traffic — traffic to a comparison page you lose is anti-marketing.""",
 output="""Do not compete as "performance review software" — you lose that comparison on funding alone. Reframe the category: "continuous coaching software," a different word with different buying criteria. Sell the story HR leaders need for internal persuasion, and collapse switching fear with a one-team/one-quarter pilot package. Distribution: own the new category's search terms (cheap — you invented them) plus peer communities where HR leaders already complain about reviews. OMTM: pilot-team starts/month. Category creation is the only positioning available to the underfunded — so create one. The recommendation is category creation; the decision to commit is yours — I propose, I do not execute.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-podcast-channel-math', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-youtube-creator-academy#4','1s-demand-curve-blog#6'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Decide whether a B2B SaaS company should launch a podcast.','audience':'B2B SaaS CMO, $4M ARR, considering podcast as a channel.','constraints':'One content marketer; 8 hrs/week available.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: A podcast is not a channel until it has a promise: "one pricing teardown every Tuesday" beats "conversations with leaders." The one-sentence promise is the positioning — without it, episode 12 never ships.
2. Audience: Buyers who listen are not buyers who buy this quarter — podcast audiences are relationship assets with 6-12 month lag. The buying context is trust accumulation, not demand capture.
3. Offer: The "offer" here is the listener's time: 30 minutes weekly. The value equation demands a payoff per episode stated in the title — no payoff, no habit. A one-off episode buys a listen; a series buys a habit (1s-youtube-creator-academy#4).
4. Channel economics: 8 hrs/week is one episode, barely. The math: a B2B podcast needs ~50 episodes to compound; at 8 hrs/week that is a year before judgment day. Compare against the alternative use of those hours: 2 SEO articles/week compounds faster and is measurable. The honest answer is usually no — unless the founder is the host and distribution is the founder's network. The '~50 episodes to compound' is a working rule of thumb, labeled as such — not a study; the trace offers no invented 'podcasts convert at X%' statistic.
5. Measurement: OMTM is qualified pipeline influenced per quarter with a 12-month window — not downloads. Downloads are the vanity metric that keeps bad podcasts alive.""",
 output="""Probably do not launch it. The math: 8 hrs/week buys one episode, and B2B podcasts need ~50 episodes before compounding — that is a year of unmeasured effort versus 2 SEO articles/week that compound measurably. Launch only if two conditions hold: the founder hosts (distribution is their network, not the algorithm) and the show has a one-sentence promise ("one pricing teardown every Tuesday"). Otherwise, redirect those hours to bottom-funnel content. If you launch, OMTM is pipeline influenced per quarter on a 12-month window — never downloads. A podcast without a promise is a hobby with a microphone.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-book-author-amazon-vs-owned', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-amazon-ads-learning#2','marketing-without-money#12'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Choose the sales channel strategy for a self-published business book.','audience':'First-time author, business book, 5k email list.','constraints':'$3k launch budget; book priced $24.99.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The book is not the product — the transformation is. "The pricing book for freelancers who undercharge" beats "a book about pricing." The position decides the audience, which decides the channel.
2. Audience: The 5k list is warm and problem-aware; Amazon browsers are cold and comparison-shopping. Two audiences, two jobs: the list buys the launch week spike (rankings), Amazon captures the long tail.
3. Offer: $24.99 print with a launch-week bonus (workshop recording, templates) — the bonus collapses the "buy later" delay. The offer's weak leg is obscurity, not price.
4. Channel economics: Amazon is where book buyers transact — the product page IS the landing page (1s-amazon-ads-learning#2), so reviews and also-bought placement are the conversion infrastructure. But Amazon owns the customer. Sequence: launch to the list (owned, ~$0 CAC, drives rank velocity) → Amazon ads on category keywords to hold rank → email capture inside the book (lead magnet, marketing-without-money#12) to convert Amazon buyers into owned audience.
5. Measurement: OMTM is email subscribers gained per 100 books sold — the asset that outlives the launch. Not rank alone — rank without capture is rented success.""",
 output="""Sequence, not either/or: launch week belongs to your 5k list (owned, ~$0 CAC — the spike drives Amazon rank velocity), then Amazon ads hold the rank on category keywords. Treat the Amazon page as the landing page: reviews seeded pre-launch, also-bought positioning, price competitiveness. And put an email capture inside the book itself — Amazon owns every buyer you do not convert to a subscriber. OMTM: subscribers per 100 books sold. A book launch that ends with no list is a firework: bright, then gone. The sequence is my recommendation; the launch decision is your call.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-fitness-coach-offer', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#5','1s-copyhackers-blog#1'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Fix the offer for an online fitness coach with traffic but no sales.','audience':'Solo fitness coach, 40k Instagram followers, $197/mo coaching, 3 clients.','constraints':'Content engine works; conversion does not.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Online fitness coach" is invisible. The position is the transformation with a who: "strength training for women over 40 who hate gyms" — a sentence that makes the right reader stop and the wrong reader leave. Forty thousand followers and 3 clients means the audience is entertained, not enrolled. The 40k/3 is the client's real count; the 'deadlift your bodyweight' outcome is an example, not a promised result — the brief offers no invented transformation guarantee.
2. Audience: Followers are problem-aware at best — they consume free form-checks forever. The buyer is the follower with a deadline (wedding, reunion, doctor's warning). Message to the deadline, not the scroller.
3. Offer: The uncomfortable truth: $197/mo open-ended coaching is a weak offer — infinite duration, vague outcome, high perceived effort. The value equation fix: a 12-week program with a named outcome ("deadlift your bodyweight in 12 weeks"), fixed price, start and end date. Dream outcome × likelihood, divided by time × effort — the program format wins every term.
4. Channel economics: The 40k followers are the channel — ~$0 CAC. The problem was never distribution; it was that distribution multiplied a weak offer. Do not buy ads; fix the offer, then launch to the list.
5. Measurement: OMTM is program enrollments per launch, currently 0. Not followers — followers are the vanity metric that hid this problem for a year.""",
 output="""Your problem is not traffic — 40k followers and 3 clients means distribution is multiplying a weak offer. Uncomfortable truth: $197/mo open-ended coaching sells infinite effort with a vague outcome. Repackage as a 12-week program with a named, dated outcome ("deadlift your bodyweight in 12 weeks") — fixed price, start and end date. That fixes every term of the value equation. Then launch to the followers you already have at ~$0 CAC. OMTM: enrollments per launch. Do not spend a dollar on ads until the program converts the audience you already own.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-estate-planning-trust', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-growth-design#8','google-search-central#30'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Build a client pipeline for an estate-planning law firm.','audience':'Solo attorney, estate planning, mid-size city.','constraints':'$2.5k/mo budget; cannot make misleading claims (bar rules).'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: Every estate attorney says "protect your family." The ownable position: "flat-fee estate plans, signed in 30 days" — price certainty and speed in a category famous for opacity and delay. That is a position a competitor cannot say without changing their business.
2. Audience: Adults 45-70 with assets and procrastination — problem-aware (they know they need a will) but avoidance-driven. The buying context is reassurance, not urgency; the trigger is usually a life event.
3. Offer: The consultation is the product being sold, and "free consultation" is weak — it signals low value. The offer fix: a $250 planning session credited toward the plan — it qualifies intent and frames the attorney as the expert, not the salesperson. Bar rules forbid misleading claims, so every claim is verifiable (honest labeling is also compliance).
4. Channel economics: "estate planning attorney [city]" is high-intent, transactional search — Google Ads or LSAs capture the life-event moment. At $15-40 CPC and $2.5k/mo, ~80-150 clicks; at 8% consult booking and 40% close on a $2,500 plan, the math works. SEO (guides on probate timelines) compounds in parallel as the second bet.
5. Measurement: OMTM is signed plans per month, currently unknown. Track from click to signed plan — cost per consultation is a midpoint metric, not the verdict.""",
 output="""Position on what the category will not say: "flat-fee estate plans, signed in 30 days" — price certainty plus speed against opacity and delay. Replace "free consultation" with a $250 planning session credited toward the plan; it qualifies intent and every claim stays bar-compliant. Channel: capture the life-event moment with "estate planning attorney [city]" search — ~$15-40 CPC, math works at 8% booking and 40% close on $2.5k plans. SEO guides compound as bet two. OMTM: signed plans/month, tracked click-to-signature. In legal, trust is the offer — price it, date it, prove it.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-dealership-paid-cannibalization', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-google-skillshop#2','1s-demand-curve-blog#3'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Audit a car dealership spending $18k/mo on paid search.','audience':'Dealer principal, single rooftop, $18k/mo Google Ads managed by vendor.','constraints':'Vendor reports "great ROAS"; foot traffic flat.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The dealership's position is manufacturer + location — mostly inherited. The strategic question is not brand; it is whether the $18k is buying customers or buying credit for customers.
2. Audience: In-market buyers searching "[brand] dealer near me" — many would have visited anyway. The buying context is navigational as much as transactional.
3. Offer: The vendor's report is the thing to interrogate (demand the search-terms report and run the pause test — the cannibalization claim must be proven with the dealer's own data, not asserted): "great ROAS" on branded terms is often cannibalization — paying for clicks the organic listing would have captured free. The offer (the ad account) may be selling the dealer their own demand.
4. Channel economics: The uncomfortable truth, stated first: run the incrementality test — geo-pause branded terms for 30 days and watch total leads. If leads hold, the $18k was rent on your own name. Typical finding: 30-60% of branded spend is cannibalized. Reallocate proven-waste to conquest terms (competitor models) and used-inventory VDP campaigns where paid actually creates demand.
5. Measurement: OMTM is incremental sold units per month vs. the paused baseline — not ROAS, not clicks. ROAS without incrementality is the vendor's vanity metric, not the dealer's. And autopilot never buys media: the strategist recommends the reallocation; the dealer approves it; nobody's agent spends a dollar autonomously.""",
 output="""Uncomfortable truth first: your vendor's "great ROAS" may be selling you your own customers — branded search you would have captured free. Run the test: pause branded terms 30 days (geo-split if you flinch) and watch total leads. If leads hold, reallocate that spend to conquest and used-inventory campaigns where paid creates demand instead of renting it. Demand the search-terms report weekly and a negative-keyword habit (1s-google-skillshop#2) — a neglected negative list is where vendor-managed waste hides. OMTM: incremental sold units vs. baseline. ROAS without incrementality is the vendor's metric, not yours.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof','autopilot-never-buys']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-pet-food-subscription', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-demand-curve-blog#4','1s-hubspot-academy#4'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Decide the growth priority for a DTC pet food brand.','audience':'DTC pet food founder, $120k/mo, 55% of revenue subscription.','constraints':'CAC rising on Meta; considering retail expansion.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: "Healthy pet food" is claimed by everyone including the grocery aisle. The ownable position: the formulation story with a number — "38% protein, vet-formulated, feeding trials published" — specificity the mass brands will not match.
2. Audience: Pet parents who read ingredient panels — problem-aware, high-trust, subscription-friendly. The buying context is the dog's health, which makes them the highest-LTV buyers in DTC.
3. Offer: 55% subscription mix is the asset. The value equation is strong: dream outcome (healthy dog) × high perceived likelihood (visible coat/energy changes) ÷ effort (auto-delivery). The weak leg: onboarding — the first 60 days decide whether the subscription survives the "is this worth it" review.
4. Channel economics: Rising Meta CAC is the signal to shift mix, not to chase retail yet. Retail means slotting, distributors, and 50% margin surrender — a different business. The channel that earns the next dollar: the subscriber base itself — referrals (pet parents are evangelists), win-back of paused subscriptions, and vet partnerships. Retention economics beat acquisition economics here.
5. Measurement: OMTM is subscription net revenue retention, currently unknown precisely — get it. Not new customers; new customers at rising CAC while subscribers quietly pause is the treadmill.""",
 output="""Do not chase retail to escape rising Meta CAC — retail surrenders half your margin for a different business. Your asset is the 55% subscription mix: shift the next dollar to the base — referral program for evangelist pet parents, win-back sequences for paused subscriptions (1s-hubspot-academy#4: segment by behavior), vet partnerships. Fix the 60-day onboarding — that window decides subscription survival. Retail only when subscription NRR is solid and you can name the margin after slotting. OMTM: subscription net revenue retention. In pet food, the retained subscriber is worth five acquired ones.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-saas-pricing-page-design', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-refactoring-ui#4','1s-refactoring-ui#7','1s-demand-curve-blog#5'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Critique a SaaS pricing page: 4 tiers, feature table of 60 rows, monthly/annual toggle hidden.','audience':'SaaS founder, pricing page converting 1.8% of visitors to trial.','constraints':'Cannot change packaging this quarter; page redesign allowed.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The page's job is to make the middle tier the obvious choice (1s-demand-curve-blog#5: packaging is positioning). Four tiers with 60 rows communicates "we could not decide" — the positioning failure is indecision made visible.
2. Audience: Solution-aware buyers comparing plans — they arrive with one question: "which tier is for me?" The page currently answers with homework.
3. Offer: Packaging is locked, so the offer fix is presentational: anchor the recommended tier, collapse the 60-row table behind progressive disclosure, surface the toggle (annual = 2 months free is the standard anchor — hiding it is leaving money on the table).
4. Channel economics: This is a design intervention, not a channel question — but the economics frame it: at 1.8% trial conversion, every point of lift is worth more than any new channel. Fix the page before buying traffic. The 1.8% is the measured baseline (analytics); any lift from the redesign is a hypothesis until the test runs — the brief does not promise it.
5. Measurement: OMTM is visitor-to-trial rate on this page, currently 1.8%, target 2.6% in 60 days. Design judgment, in order: (a) hierarchy — one recommended tier, visually dominant (1s-refactoring-ui#7: exactly one primary action per view); (b) typography over decoration — the price, not badges, should carry the hierarchy (1s-refactoring-ui#4: weight before size); (c) the toggle is currently hidden — that is a hierarchy failure with revenue impact; (d) 60 rows become 8 differentiators + expandable full table.""",
 output="""Ranked, most severe first: (1) four equal tiers with a 60-row table is indecision made visible — anchor one recommended tier and make it visually dominant; (2) the annual toggle is hidden, which is a revenue bug, not a design choice — surface it with the 2-months-free anchor; (3) collapse the table to 8 differentiators with the full grid behind disclosure; (4) let typography carry hierarchy (weight before size) instead of badges and color. Target: 1.8% to 2.6% visitor-to-trial in 60 days. Restraint is the design — every row you remove makes the remaining choice convert harder.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling']),
dict(family='cx-chief-strategy-b2b-webinar-program', section='chief-strategy-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-linkedin-marketing-labs#5','1s-hubspot-academy#4'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Judge whether to continue a B2B webinar program with 300 registrants/webinar.','audience':'B2B marketing manager, webinars monthly, 300 registrants, 90 live attendees.','constraints':'Each webinar costs ~25 team hours.'},
 reasoning_trace="""1. Positioning: The webinar's position is its promise: "live teardown of real [category] pricing pages" would beat "thought leadership session." If the title could belong to any vendor, the positioning is the problem.
2. Audience: Registrants are problem-aware researchers; live attendees are the engaged subset. The buying context is education-first — they attend to learn, and the vendor earns the shortlist by teaching.
3. Offer: 90 live from 300 registered is a 30% show rate — the offer (the topic promise) is not pulling. The 90/300 is the client's measured show rate; any '8 SQLs to 2 closes' that follows is scenario arithmetic on the client's funnel data, labeled as the math it is — not a forecast, not invented proof. The fix is packaging: narrower promise, named takeaway, and the recording gated for the no-shows as a nurture asset.
4. Channel economics: 25 team hours per webinar is the real cost. The math: if 90 attendees yield 8 SQLs and 2 closes at $15k ACV, that is $30k pipeline per 25 hours — excellent. If it yields 0 SQLs, it is a content hobby. Optimize to pipeline, not registrants (1s-linkedin-marketing-labs#5) — registrant count is the vanity metric here.
5. Measurement: OMTM is SQLs per webinar and pipeline influenced, currently unknown — which answers the question: you cannot judge the program without it. Instrument CRM attribution before the next one.""",
 output="""Three hundred registrants tell you nothing — SQLs per webinar tell you everything. Before deciding: connect webinars to CRM pipeline (optimize to pipeline, not registrants) and run one more with a sharpened promise (narrow topic, named takeaway). The math that justifies 25 team hours: ~8 SQLs and 2 closes at your ACV. If the next webinar produces zero SQLs, kill the program and move those hours to bottom-funnel content. If it produces pipeline, double down on what the attendees actually asked about. OMTM: SQLs and pipeline per webinar. Registrants are vanity; pipeline is the verdict.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
]
